{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Who struck you on the way.</b> A term of chance encounter. Another interpretation — a term of emission and impurity, that he would render them impure through male lie. Another interpretation — a term of cold and heat: he cooled you off and melted your boiling ardor — for all the nations were afraid to do battle with you, and this one came and began, and showed a place to others. A parable: a boiling bath that no creature can enter — a worthless fellow came, jumped, and descended into it; even though he was scalded, he cooled it for others (Tanchuma)." "<b>And he struck your tail.</b> The blow of the tail — he would cut off the circumcisions and fling them upward (Tanchuma)." "<b>All who lagged behind you.</b> Those lacking strength on account of their sin, for the cloud would expel them (Tanchuma)." "<b>And you were faint and weary.</b> Faint from thirst — as it is written ״וַיִּצְמָא שָׁם הָעָם לַמַּיִם״ (Exodus 17), and afterward it is written ״וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק״." "<b>And weary.</b> From the way." "<b>and did not fear.</b> Amalek did not fear <b>Elohim</b> — from harming you."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:48:26.821042789Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}